{{Nihongo|'''''Godzilla Minus Zero'''''|ゴジラ{{ruby-ja|-0.0|マイナスゼロ}}|Gojira Mainasu Zero|lead=yes}} is a 2026 Japanese [[Epic film|epic]] ''[[kaiju]]'' film written, directed, and with visual effects by [[Takashi Yamazaki]]. A sequel to ''[[Godzilla Minus One]]'' (2023), it is the 39th film in the [[Godzilla (franchise)|''Godzilla'' franchise]], the 34th film thereof produced by [[Toho]], and the sixth installment in the [[Godzilla (franchise)#Reiwa era (2016–present)|''Reiwa'' era]].{{efn|Japan's [[Reiwa era|''Reiwa'' era]] began on May 1, 2019,<ref name="CNN">{{cite web |url=https://www.cnn.com/2019/03/31/asia/japan-new-era-reiwa-intl/index.html |title='Reiwa': Japan announces dawn of a new era |last1=McKirdy |first1=Euan |author2=Junko Ogura |last3=Griffiths |first3=James |work=[[CNN]] |date=April 1, 2019|access-date=November 4, 2025 |url-status=live|archive-date=October 12, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191012071316/https://www.cnn.com/2019/03/31/asia/japan-new-era-reiwa-intl/index.html}}</ref> however, Toho considers ''[[Shin Godzilla]]'' (2016) and the [[Polygon Pictures]] anime trilogy – ''[[Godzilla: Planet of the Monsters]]'' (2017), ''[[Godzilla: City on the Edge of Battle]]'', and ''[[Godzilla: The Planet Eater]]'' (both 2018) – as part of the ''Reiwa'' era.<ref>{{cite book |last=Skipper |first=Graham |others=[[Toho]] |title=Godzilla: The Official Guide to the King of the Monsters |date=September 15, 2022 |publisher=[[Welbeck Publishing Group]] |page=217 |isbn=978-1787398993 }}</ref>}} The film stars [[Ryunosuke Kamiki]], [[Minami Hamabe]], [[Hidetaka Yoshioka]], [[Yuki Yamada (actor)|Yuki Yamada]], [[Kuranosuke Sasaki]], [[Sakura Ando]], and [[Miou Tanaka]] reprising their roles from ''Minus One'', with [[Min Tanaka]], [[Masami Nagasawa]], and [[Jun Kunimura]] also starring as new characters. Set in 1949, the film shows [[postwar Japan]] struggling to rebuild as it faces a new threat: [[King Ghidorah]]. [[Godzilla (Takashi Yamazaki)|Godzilla]] reappears after fully regenerating from his previous defeat and is drawn into conflict with the three-headed monster, guided through human intervention.

Following the international success of ''Godzilla Minus One'', Toho announced in November 2024 that another Yamazaki-helmed ''Godzilla'' film had been [[Greenlight|greenlit]]. By February 2025, Yamazaki had begun screenwriting and storyboarding the follow-up. In April 2025, [[Toho International]]'s president, Kōji Ueda, clarified that the new film would be a sequel, planned for a 2026 release. [[Principal photography]] commenced in August 2025 and [[Wrap (filmmaking)|wrapped]] by December. It became the first Japanese production [[List of IMAX films|filmed for IMAX]]. The film's title was announced in November 2025. [[Shirogumi]] handled the visual effects.

''Godzilla Minus Zero'' premiered at the [[2026 New York Film Festival]] on September 26, and was released theatrically in Japan by Toho on November 3. Toho's subsidiary [[GKIDS]] later released the film in the United States and Canada on November 6.<!-- Do NOT change this to "critical acclaim" unless multiple reliable sources referring to its reception as that are found per [[MOS:ACCLAIMED]] --> The film received critical acclaim for its visual effects, spectacle, sound, Naoki Satō’s score, Yamazaki’s direction, human story, themes, action, and King Ghidorah's visual representation. However, its pacing received some criticism, leaving critics and audiences divided on whether the overall film was superior to its predecessor.<ref name="Davids2026-1" /><ref name="RT">{{Cite web |title=''Godzilla Minus Zero'' First Reviews: Bigger, Bolder, and Somehow More Visually Impressive than ''Minus One'' |url=https://editorial.rottentomatoes.com/article/godzilla-minus-zero-first-reviews/ |access-date=September 28, 2026 |website=[[Rotten Tomatoes]] |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260928062419/https://editorial.rottentomatoes.com/article/godzilla-minus-zero-first-reviews/ |archive-date=September 28, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> The film has grossed $120.5 million ({{JPY|19.2 billion}}) worldwide,<ref name="CinemaToday"/> setting records upon opening for the highest-grossing opening day for any live-action film in Japan, the top opening weekend for a foreign-language live-action film in the U.S. and Canada, and the biggest [[IMAX]] global opening for a Japanese film. It went on to dethrone its predecessor as the highest-grossing Japanese ''Godzilla'' film of all time.<ref name="CinemaToday"/>

== Plot ==
<!-- Please see WP:FILMPLOT for more information. The word count in this section should be kept between 400-700 words. The plot summary does NOT intend to reproduce the experience of watching, nor is it meant to cover EVERY detail. It should serve as an overview of the major plot elements and events. PER WP:FILMPLOT, PLOT SUMMARIES FOR FEATURE FILMS AND TELEVISION SERIES SHOULD BE BETWEEN 400 AND 700 WORDS. See "Wikipedia:How to write a plot summary" (WP:PLOTSUM) for general guidelines.

Current word count is 659 words, correct as of 25/11/2026.-->
In 1949, two years after [[Godzilla (Takashi Yamazaki)|Godzilla's]] apparent death in [[Sagami Bay]],{{Efn|As depicted in ''[[Godzilla Minus One]]'' (2023)}} Japan continues to [[Postwar Japan|recover from the war]] and Godzilla's attacks. Former ''[[kamikaze]]'' pilot Kōichi Shikishima has married Noriko Ōishi, a survivor of Godzilla's [[Ginza]] attack, who has begun to display physical mutations as a result of exposure to Godzilla's cells ("G-cells"), together raising their adopted daughter Akiko, while naval engineer Kenji Noda is now the director of the Large-Scale Disaster Response Bureau, an organization created to prepare for future threats.

[[Matsumoto, Nagano|Matsumoto]] is attacked by a [[European dragon]]-like creature capable of flight and firing electrical beams from its mouth. Professor Kanji Murakami, a survivor of the [[Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki#Hiroshima|bombing of Hiroshima]] and Godzilla's attack on [[Ginza]], and his assistant, Akira Hagiwara, document the attack and report their findings to Noda. The creature arrived in a meteorite as a gelatinous organism before consuming local wildlife and adopting its current form. Combining the words "[[Chimera (mythology)|chimera]]" and "[[Lernaean Hydra|hydra]]", they name it "[[King Ghidorah|Ghidorah]]". Meteorologist Makoto Aoyama tracks Ghidorah through weather patterns, as it seems to be attracted to lightning storms.

Kōichi volunteers to lead an attack against Ghidorah while it is dormant; the United States reluctantly contributes a [[Thermobaric weapon|fuel-air bomb]] to be dropped from a [[Nakajima G5N Shinzan]] bomber escorted by Japanese-piloted [[Douglas A-1 Skyraider]]s. Ghidorah survives the explosion and kills the other escort pilots. Kōichi distracts Ghidorah as the bomber deploys harpoons equipped with "[[Raijin|Narukami]]" [[Lightning rod|lightning rods]] into its back, attracting a series of lightning strikes that seemingly kill it. However, Ghidorah revives and evolves into a larger, three-headed, double-tailed monster, gains the ability to lift and smash buildings with its beam, and continues attacking cities across Japan, killing thousands.

As Ghidorah continues its rampage, Godzilla completes its regeneration and resurfaces near [[Kōzu-shima]]. Murakami argues that forcing the monsters to fight each other is the only way to defeat them, regarding Godzilla's return as "divine providence", and formulates a plan to lure Godzilla to Ghidorah using a Resonance Wave Amplifier, a device which is attached to an organism to attract others of the same species. However, Murakami secretly plots to have Godzilla destroy the U.S. to avenge his family, who died in Hiroshima, by using the amplifier to draw Godzilla to [[New York City]]. As Murakami's plan requires Noriko to operate the amplifier due to her genetic mutations, Kōichi flees with his family to prevent Noriko from being used as a weapon. However, after they pass through the devastated Matsumoto, Noriko chooses to volunteer for the plan, and they return to Tokyo.

The bureau builds generators and traps in the ruins of Ginza to distract Ghidorah, while Kōichi pilots Murakami's [[Kawanishi H8K]] seaplane to lure Godzilla with Noriko and the amplifier on board. Godzilla and Ghidorah are successfully goaded into a confrontation and Godzilla manages to sever one of Ghidorah's heads, but Ghidorah ultimately incapacitates it, burying it beneath rubble. The bureau manages to freeze Ghidorah with [[liquid nitrogen]] before covering it with fuel to create a deadly temperature fluctuation. When the trap's fuel lines are severed, Noda activates it manually. As he drives away, Ghidorah strikes his vehicle with an electrical beam, causing it to crash. Kōichi fires a rocket from his aircraft into Ghidorah to ignite the fuel, inadvertently killing Noda; to spare Kōichi further guilt, Aoyama instead tells him Noda was killed by Ghidorah. Kōichi and Noriko use the amplifier to resuscitate Godzilla, while Ghidorah reattaches its severed head, absorbing some of Godzilla's G-cells, and gains the ability to use its energy beam. The monsters unleash their energy beams, but Godzilla's beam hits Ghidorah first, and Ghidorah is killed.

After Godzilla's victory, the U.S. military begins bombarding it, but ceases fire after being ordered to preserve it for potential future use as a weapon. Aoyama relays the orders to the Japanese troops whilst secretly warning the seaplane crew to flee. Murakami, whom Noda persuaded against using Godzilla as a weapon and continuing the cycle of killing, burns his research notes and destroys the amplifier. He attempts to flee with Hagiwara, but they are apprehended by American soldiers. Kōichi and Noriko, realizing that the military could also exploit Noriko's G-cells, prepare to go into hiding and leave Akiko at the war orphanage Noriko runs with their neighbor, Sumiko Ōta. However, they ultimately relent and take Akiko with them, while Godzilla returns to the sea.

